<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vardar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Axios</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vardar is the longest river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, about 390 kilometres long, flowing from the Šar Mountains past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, where as the Axios it enters the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>. Its valley has long been the main route between the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and the Aegean. Historians often divide the region of Macedonia into Vardar, Aegean and Pirin Macedonia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vardar.jpg" alt="Vardar" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: kallerna · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vardar_Skopje_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The lowland plains around Pella and along the Haliacmon and <b>Axius</b> rivers produced grain, the uplands supported flocks, and the forests supplied ship timber and pitch, a royal resource coveted by every Hellenistic navy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Drougoubitai settled in the plain west and north-west of Thessalonica, towards the <b>Vardar</b> and Pelagonia, in the early 7th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/drougoubitai">Drougoubitai</a></li><li><blockquote>The Paeonians held the <b>Axios</b> and Strymon valleys from early times; Homer lists them as allies of Troy.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-paeonia">Kingdom of Paeonia</a></li><li><blockquote>The brothers ruled from Velbazhd and Strumica, taxing the trade routes between the <b>Vardar</b> and the Struma.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-dejanovic">Lordship of Dejanović</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1197 he seized the fortress of Prosek, in the Demir Kapija gorge of the <b>Vardar</b>, and nearby Strumica.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-prosek">Principality of Prosek</a></li></ul></section>
